A||||5||jeffndebrus||Alternator bracket replacing--engine mount slipped||||9||
Z||000000||jeffndebrus||10-17-2001||07:57 PM||||Please help me friends.<BR>While dreaming about the 3800 I will have someday soon---I was replacing the alternator bracket which had broken in half on my 2.8 when I ran into a problem.<BR>Seems that the bottom bolt would not thread so I pulled the bracket out and looked at the hole--guess what-- the engine has shifted and the bolt does not line up.  Any ideas on how I might fix this would be appreciated.||||reg||9
Z||000001||Fiero*101||10-17-2001||10:54 PM||||Hmmm what bolt doesnt line up?<BR>did you try to fix the Bracket?<BR>more info needed<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Fiero*101<BR>Gold 86 SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000002||jeffndebrus||10-18-2001||08:53 AM||||Lying on your back under the passenger side of the car,  it is the top bolt on the rear engine mount.  The mount itself looks like a letter L with two bolts verticle through the frame and two bolts that go horizontal into the engine block.  The top one that goes through the alternator bracket before going into the engine block is the one in question.  It appears that either the engine moved or the mont moved. I cannot bolt my alternator bracket back on because this top bolt no longer lines up with the engine.  It is only off about 1/8"-- frustrating-- this is the thing that keeps me from driving my recently aquired fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||10-18-2001||03:22 PM||||I had a similar thing happen with my 85GT.  I used a floor jack under the oil pan (use a piece of 2x6 to spread the load) and carefully lifted the engine a bit to line up the bolt holes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||jeffndebrus||10-18-2001||07:12 PM||||is it safe to jack the oil pan- i would hate to destroy it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||10-18-2001||09:16 PM||||Use a piece of wood between the jack and the pan and you should be OK.  You're not going to be lifting it very much to get the holes to line up.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||14
